  "conclusions": [
    "Correctness gates pass: frozen CPU and Metal coarse produce exact outputs; same-rescue full search has exact seeds/final outputs; 6/12 scenes pass calibrated truth; both 8192-square tests detect all six objects in first plus every measured call with zero geometry error.",
    "Joint full-search Src7 and six-object median gains are only 0.10% and 1.40%, with overlapping five-call ranges; treat as unchanged or inconclusive. Twelve-object joint search improves 6.35% with disjoint ranges in this run, not a universal guarantee.",
    "Standalone twelve-object apparent 18.45% gain is confounded by CPU within-run drift (approximately 738 to 505 ms); do not use as the headline acceleration result.",
    "Both 8192-square fixed-angle cases show no useful speed improvement (Metal medians 2.44% and 2.09% higher, overlapping ranges). CPU stages outside top NCC remain 97.1% and 97.7% of Metal wall time.",
    "Shared-buffer copies are below 0.06% of total wall in every representative case. Data copying is not the measured bottleneck of this implementation.",
    "Small top-level maps issue 40 or 140 synchronous GPU commands per search. Residual submission/wait cost exceeds command GPU duration on all three joint real cases; batching angles or eliminating repeated waits is a supported next experiment, not a demonstrated gain.",
    "No per-stage CPU baseline instrumentation exists, so these reports do not identify an isolated CPU-NCC-to-Metal kernel speedup or separately quantify pyramid versus rotation versus fine-search cost.",
    "Retain opt-in selection. The experiment validates exactness and identifies a workload-specific benefit; it does not justify enabling GPU coarse search everywhere or claiming the entire coarse pipeline is GPU-resident."
  ]
}
